    // read, never typed: the range comes from the ladder's own first and last
    // rung, and the threshold from WINDOW_THRESHOLD, which is also what
    // tierFor() reads.
    lead: `One rate per product, and it falls as the count rises — from ${euro(ladderRate('complete', 1), 'en')} down to ${euro(ladderFloor('complete'), 'en')} a product. From ${WINDOW_THRESHOLD} products the order goes into the studio calendar and gets a reserved window; below that it runs in the standard queue.`,
    // Both service levels read this, so it has to be true of both. Section 13
    // asks for the difference between them to be visible rather than hidden,
    // and the honest way to hold that here is to name what each one gets, in
    // the same breath, and let the contrast do the work. What changed under the
    // ladder is only what decides which you get: the count, not a radio.
    gateLead: 'No date is printed until the calendar clears it.',
    gateBody: `From ${WINDOW_THRESHOLD} products an order is put in the capacity calendar and gets a reserved window, confirmed in writing before you pay and never after. A smaller order runs in the standard queue, with no date attached at all. Neither answer is an optimistic guess.`,
    sharedH: 'What is identical at every count',
    shared: [
      ['The pipeline', `The same production run at one product and at ${ATTENDED_PER_WINDOW}. There is no second-rate lane.`],
      ['The review', `${reviewClaim('unattended', 'en')}. At every count, no exceptions.`],
      ['The files', 'High-resolution and e-commerce-ready. Yours to use anywhere, with no per-use licence.'],
    ],
